"cell_type":"In","gene_symbol":"PTGDS","gene_symbol_current":"PTGDS","gene_id":"ENSG00000107317","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":0.897039,"nucleus_log2fc":0.0518204,"nucleus_score":0.389753,"pct_ad":0.110914,"pct_control":0.107376,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.999889,"donor_log2fc":0.327776,"donor_lfcse":0.234332,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":6.0371e-06},{"cell_type":"OPC","gene_symbol":"PTGDS","gene_symbol_current":"PTGDS","gene_id":"ENSG00000107317","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"lower in AD","nucleus_padj":0.780874,"nucleus_log2fc":-0.050551,"nucleus_score":-0.60827,"pct_ad":0.318681,"pct_control":0.335632,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.986209,"donor_log2fc":0.153733,"donor_lfcse":0.213075,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.000681691},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_symbol":"PTGDS","gene_symbol_current":"PTGDS","gene_id":"ENSG00000107317","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":true,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":0.031789,"nucleus_log2fc":0.0971908,"nucleus_score":2.46956,"pct_ad":0.648463,"pct_control":0.630506,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.903597,"donor_log2fc":0.0832911,"donor_lfcse":0.117808,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.00557262}],"modules":[{"cell_type":"Ast","gene_id":"ENSG00000107317","module":"turquoise","kme":0.221488,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.32433,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"In","gene_id":"ENSG00000107317","module":"blue","kme":0.158685,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.906476,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"OPC","gene_id":"ENSG00000107317","module":"turquoise","kme":0.289028,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.704234,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_id":"ENSG00000107317","module":"turquoise","kme":0.309579,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.415372,"module_significant":false}],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000107317","entrez_id":["5730"],"uniprot":["P41222"],"ncbi_summary":"The protein encoded by this gene is a glutathione-independent prostaglandin D synthase that catalyzes the conversion of prostaglandin H2 (PGH2) to postaglandin D2 (PGD2). PGD2 functions as a neuromodulator as well as a trophic factor in the central nervous system. PGD2 is also involved in smooth muscle contraction/relaxation and is a potent inhibitor of platelet aggregation. This gene is preferentially expressed in brain. Studies with transgenic mice overexpressing this gene suggest that this gene may be also involved in the regulation of non-rapid eye movement sleep. [provided by RefSeq, Jul 2008].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"P41222","text":"Catalyzes the conversion of PGH2 to PGD2, a prostaglandin involved in smooth muscle contraction/relaxation and a potent inhibitor of platelet aggregation (PubMed:20667974). Involved in a variety of CNS functions, such as sedation, NREM sleep and PGE2-induced allodynia, and may have an anti-apoptotic role in oligodendrocytes. Binds small non-substrate lipophilic molecules, including biliverdin, bilirubin, retinal, retinoic acid and thyroid hormone, and may act as a scavenger for harmful hydrophobic molecules and as a secretory retinoid and thyroid hormone transporter. Possibly involved in development and maintenance of the blood-brain, blood-retina, blood-aqueous humor and blood-testis barrier. It is likely to play important roles in both maturation and maintenance of the central nervous system and male reproductive system (PubMed:20667974, PubMed:9475419). Involved in PLA2G3-dependent maturation of mast cells. PLA2G3 is secreted by immature mast cells and acts on nearby fibroblasts upstream to PTDGS to synthesize PGD2, which in turn promotes mast cell maturation and degranulation via PTGDR (By similarity). Can also, catalyze the conversion of PGH3 (derived from eicosapentaenoate (EPA, C20:5n-3)) to PGD3, though PGH2 remains the preferred substrate (PubMed:17519235)."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_03","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}